, 			{
				"box" : 				{
					"id" : "obj-60",
					"linecount" : 5,
					"maxclass" : "comment",
					"numinlets" : 1,
					"numoutlets" : 0,
					"patching_rect" : [ 1969.333392024040222, 582.000020503997803, 531.0, 74.0 ],
					"text" : "## Recording\n\nPlease make a short recording of your instrument using your mobile phone. Don’t worry about polishing or editing the video. Make sure to capture all the interactions you defined, including the ultrasonic sensor, the potentiometer, and the button(s)."
				}

			}
, 			{
				"box" : 				{
					"id" : "obj-61",
					"linecount" : 10,
					"maxclass" : "comment",
					"numinlets" : 1,
					"numoutlets" : 0,
					"patching_rect" : [ 1962.666725158691406, 431.333349347114563, 531.0, 141.0 ],
					"text" : "Repository\nCreate a new repository for your instrument or add the instrument files to your Game Controller repo. Your repo must:\n\n- Include your `.ino` and your `.maxpat` files, as well as your Live or Logic sessions (including audio files) if you worked with those programs.\n- Include `README.md` file providing a simple description of the expected instrument interactions.\n- Make proper use of the `.gitignore` file.\n    - I suggest using the MacOS [.gitignore](https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/Global/macOS.gitignore)."
				}

			}
, 			{
				"box" : 				{
					"id" : "obj-4",
					"linecount" : 16,
					"maxclass" : "comment",
					"numinlets" : 1,
					"numoutlets" : 0,
					"patching_rect" : [ 1956.00005829334259, 168.666674852371216, 537.0, 234.0 ],
					"text" : "- Re-Mapping\n    - For your button, remember you can apply simple arithmetic operations to output values other than 0 and 1.\n    - You can also use `sel` object paired with message boxes to output any values of your choice.\n- Interpolation\n    - For your potentiometer and your ultrasonic sensor, consider the input range that you’d like to map to an output range.\n    - Some things to remember:\n        - Check for minimum and maximum values of the range.\n        - Check mapping ranges and interpolation coefficients.\n            - You may work with `zmap` or `scale`.\n            - Recall that `scale` has a fifth parameter that can be used to define an interpolation coefficient. If this parameter isn’t provided, the resulting interpolation will be linear.\n        - Since the DAC of the Teensy board uses 10 bits to represent data, the range of the potentiometer will be 0-1023.\n\n"
				}

			}
, 			{
				"box" : 				{
